# MLServe: Versioning and Canary Rollout

```elixir
Mix.install([
  # Running from a clone of the repo. Against the published package this is:
  #   {:ml_serve, "~> 0.1.0"}
  {:ml_serve, path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)},
  {:kino, "~> 0.14"}
])
```

## The problem

Shipping a new model is not shipping new code. The code is identical; the weights changed. Yet
the usual deployment story forces the two together: rebuild, redeploy, restart, and hope, with
no way to send 5% of traffic to the new weights and compare.

MLServe keys models by `{name, version}`, so two versions of the same model run side by side in
one node, each with its own supervision subtree, its own workers and its own counters. Routing
between them is an ETS read, which makes promotion a write rather than a deploy.

## Two generations

```elixir
defmodule Recommender do
  @behaviour MLServe.Model

  @impl true
  def capabilities, do: %{concurrency: :shared, load: :once}

  @impl true
  def load(config), do: {:ok, Keyword.fetch!(config, :generation)}

  @impl true
  def predict(generation, user_id) do
    {:ok, %{user: user_id, picks: Enum.map(1..3, &(&1 * generation)), generation: generation}}
  end
end
```

Load both. The second `load_model/2` does not disturb the first in any way — it is a separate
subtree that happens to share a name.

```elixir
{:ok, _} =
  MLServe.load_model(:recommender, backend: Recommender, version: "1.0.0", config: [generation: 1])

{:ok, _} =
  MLServe.load_model(:recommender, backend: Recommender, version: "2.0.0", config: [generation: 2])

:ok = MLServe.await_ready(:recommender)

MLServe.versions(:recommender)
```

## Where unpinned traffic goes

The default version is the first one loaded, until you promote another. Pinning with `:version`
bypasses default routing and any canary entirely — useful for A/B harnesses, for replaying one
request against a specific version, and for backfills that have to stay reproducible.

```elixir
{:ok, unpinned} = MLServe.predict(:recommender, "user-1")
{:ok, pinned} = MLServe.predict(:recommender, "user-1", version: "2.0.0")

%{unpinned: unpinned.generation, pinned_to_2: pinned.generation}
```

## Watching the split

Every prediction carries `:version` and `:canary?` in its telemetry metadata. That is the whole
mechanism: comparing a candidate against the incumbent needs no extra instrumentation from you,
and works identically whether your metrics backend is the ETS table below or Datadog.

```elixir
defmodule Rollout do
  def start do
    :ets.new(:rollout, [:public, :named_table])

    :telemetry.attach(
      "rollout",
      [:ml_serve, :prediction, :stop],
      &__MODULE__.handle_event/4,
      nil
    )
  end

  def handle_event(_event, _measurements, %{version: version, canary?: canary?}, _config) do
    :ets.update_counter(:rollout, {version, canary?}, {2, 1}, {{version, canary?}, 0})
    :ok
  end

  def table do
    total = :ets.tab2list(:rollout) |> Enum.map(&elem(&1, 1)) |> Enum.sum()

    for {{version, canary?}, count} <- Enum.sort(:ets.tab2list(:rollout)) do
      %{
        version: version,
        route: if(canary?, do: "canary", else: "default"),
        requests: count,
        share: "#{Float.round(count / max(total, 1) * 100, 1)}%"
      }
    end
  end

  def reset, do: :ets.delete_all_objects(:rollout)
end

Rollout.start()
```

## A canary at 20%

Each request rolls independently in the calling process. There is no coordinator and no shared
counter to contend on, so a canary costs nothing and cannot become a bottleneck.

```elixir
:ok = MLServe.canary(:recommender, "2.0.0", 20)
Rollout.reset()

for i <- 1..1_000, do: MLServe.predict!(:recommender, "user-#{i}")

Kino.DataTable.new(Rollout.table())
```

Roughly one request in five landed on the candidate. Because both rows carry a version, error
rate and latency per version are already in your metrics — which is what turns promote-or-abort
into a decision rather than a guess.

## A candidate that cannot load

The failure worth designing for is not a candidate that is worse. It is a candidate that does
not work at all.

```elixir
defmodule BrokenModel do
  @behaviour MLServe.Model

  @impl true
  def capabilities, do: %{concurrency: :shared, load: :once}

  @impl true
  def load(_config), do: {:error, :weights_file_corrupt}

  @impl true
  def predict(_state, _input), do: {:ok, :never_reached}
end
```

A model that fails to load retries with exponential backoff before being marked `:failed`, with
the reason preserved. Expect warnings in the cell below — they are the backoff, and they are the
point.

```elixir
{:ok, _} = MLServe.load_model(:recommender, backend: BrokenModel, version: "3.0.0")

# Long enough for the retry budget to be exhausted.
Process.sleep(1_200)

{:ok, status} = MLServe.model_status(:recommender, version: "3.0.0")
%{status: status.status, failure: status.failure}
```

Now send it half of all traffic anyway. Routing checks readiness per request, so the canary's
share falls back to the default version instead of erroring: a bad candidate cannot take
production down, even if you point traffic straight at it.

```elixir
:ok = MLServe.canary(:recommender, "3.0.0", 50)
Rollout.reset()

for i <- 1..500, do: MLServe.predict!(:recommender, "user-#{i}")

Kino.DataTable.new(Rollout.table())
```

Every one of the 500 was served by a ready version. None reached `3.0.0`.

## Promotion

Promotion is a single ETS write. In-flight requests finish on whatever version they started on;
the next request routes to the new default. No restart, no dropped connection, no deploy. It
also clears any active canary, because a promoted version is no longer a candidate.

```elixir
:ok = MLServe.clear_canary(:recommender)
:ok = MLServe.promote(:recommender, "2.0.0")
Rollout.reset()

for i <- 1..200, do: MLServe.predict!(:recommender, "user-#{i}")

Kino.DataTable.new(Rollout.table())
```

## Draining the old version

`unload_model/2` waits up to `:drain_timeout` for in-flight requests before tearing the subtree
down. The `[:ml_serve, :model, :unload]` event reports how many were still running when the wait
expired — `0` is a clean drain.

```elixir
{:ok, old} = MLServe.model_status(:recommender, version: "1.0.0")
served = old.requests

:ok = MLServe.unload_model(:recommender, version: "3.0.0")
:ok = MLServe.unload_model(:recommender, version: "1.0.0")

{:ok, remaining} = MLServe.versions(:recommender)
%{"1.0.0 served" => served, remaining: remaining}
```

## The full rollout, in five calls

```elixir
# 1. Load the candidate beside the incumbent.
#    MLServe.load_model(:recommender, backend: Recommender, version: "2.0.0", config: [...])
#
# 2. Send it a slice of real traffic.
#    MLServe.canary(:recommender, "2.0.0", 5)
#
# 3. Compare per-version error rate and latency in your existing metrics. No new plumbing.
#
# 4. Ship it — or abort with clear_canary/1, which costs one ETS write and no restart.
#    MLServe.promote(:recommender, "2.0.0")
#
# 5. Drain the old one.
#    MLServe.unload_model(:recommender, version: "1.0.0")

:ok = MLServe.unload_model(:recommender, version: "2.0.0")
MLServe.models()
```
